## Coproduct in the Category of Discrete Dynamical Systems

**The category DDS.** An object is a pair $(s, f)$ where $s$ is a set and $f: s \to s$ is an endofunction. A morphism $h: (s, f) \to (t, g)$ is a function $h: s \to t$ with $g \circ h = h \circ f$, i.e. the square

```
s --f--> s
|         |
h         h
v         v
t --g--> t
```

commutes. Composition and identities are those of **Set**; the commutativity condition is preserved by composition and identities, so DDS is a (concrete) category.

---

### Construction of the coproduct

Given two DDSs $(s, f)$ and $(t, g)$, form the disjoint union of the underlying sets

$$s \sqcup t \;=\; (\{0\}\times s)\,\cup\,(\{1\}\times t),$$

with the canonical injections

$$\iota_s : s \to s\sqcup t,\quad x\mapsto (0,x), \qquad \iota_t : t \to s\sqcup t,\quad y\mapsto (1,y).$$

Define the endofunction $f\sqcup g : s\sqcup t \to s\sqcup t$ piecewise:

$$(f\sqcup g)(0,x) = (0, f(x)), \qquad (f\sqcup g)(1,y) = (1, g(y)).$$

Equivalently, $f\sqcup g$ is the unique function making both squares

```
s --f--> s              t --g--> t
|         |             |         |
ι_s       ι_s           ι_t       ι_t
v         v             v         v
s⊔t --f⊔g--> s⊔t        s⊔t --f⊔g--> s⊔t
```

commute, i.e. $(f\sqcup g)\circ \iota_s = \iota_s\circ f$ and $(f\sqcup g)\circ \iota_t = \iota_t\circ g$.

**Claim.** $((s\sqcup t),\, f\sqcup g)$, together with $\iota_s, \iota_t$, is the coproduct of $(s,f)$ and $(t,g)$ in DDS.

### Universal property

For any DDS $(u, h)$ and any pair of DDS-morphisms

$$\alpha : (s,f)\to (u,h), \qquad \beta : (t,g)\to (u,h),$$

(i.e. $h\circ\alpha = \alpha\circ f$ and $h\circ\beta = \beta\circ g$), there exists a **unique** DDS-morphism

$$[\alpha,\beta] : (s\sqcup t,\, f\sqcup g) \longrightarrow (u, h)$$

with $[\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_s = \alpha$ and $[\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_t = \beta$.

### Proof of the universal property

**Existence.** Define $[\alpha,\beta] : s\sqcup t \to u$ by the universal property of the disjoint union in **Set**:

$$[\alpha,\beta](0,x)=\alpha(x), \qquad [\alpha,\beta](1,y)=\beta(y).$$

By construction $[\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_s = \alpha$ and $[\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_t = \beta$. It remains to verify it is a DDS-morphism, i.e. $h\circ[\alpha,\beta] = [\alpha,\beta]\circ(f\sqcup g)$. Check on each summand:

- On $s$: 
$$h\circ[\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_s = h\circ\alpha = \alpha\circ f = [\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_s\circ f = [\alpha,\beta]\circ(f\sqcup g)\circ\iota_s,$$
using that $\alpha$ is a DDS-morphism and $(f\sqcup g)\circ\iota_s = \iota_s\circ f$.

- On $t$: 
$$h\circ[\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_t = h\circ\beta = \beta\circ g = [\alpha,\beta]\circ\iota_t\circ g = [\alpha,\beta]\circ(f\sqcup g)\circ\iota_t,$$
using that $\beta$ is a DDS-morphism and $(f\sqcup g)\circ\iota_t = \iota_t\circ g$.

Since $s\sqcup t$ is the union of the images of $\iota_s$ and $\iota_t$, the two equalities imply $h\circ[\alpha,\beta] = [\alpha,\beta]\circ(f\sqcup g)$ on all of $s\sqcup t$. Hence $[\alpha,\beta]$ is a DDS-morphism.

**Uniqueness.** Suppose $\gamma : (s\sqcup t, f\sqcup g)\to (u,h)$ is any DDS-morphism with $\gamma\circ\iota_s = \alpha$ and $\gamma\circ\iota_t = \beta$. As a function $s\sqcup t\to u$, $\gamma$ is determined by its values on the images of $\iota_s$ and $\iota_t$, which cover $s\sqcup t$. The two equations force $\gamma(0,x)=\alpha(x)$ and $\gamma(1,y)=\beta(y)$, so $\gamma = [\alpha,\beta]$. ∎

---

### Remark (categorical context)

DDS is the functor category $\mathbf{Set}^{\mathbb{N}}$ (equivalently, the category of presheaves / algebras for the free-monoid-on-one-generator monad), where $\mathbb{N}$ is the one-object category generated by the single arrow corresponding to $f$. Coproducts in any functor category into a cocomplete category are computed pointwise. Since **Set** has coproducts (disjoint unions), the coproduct in DDS is computed pointwise: on the single object of $\mathbb{N}$ we take the disjoint union of the underlying sets, and the action of the generator is the disjoint union of the two endofunctions. This recovers exactly the construction above, confirming it is the categorical coproduct and not merely a candidate.

